# XCU awk
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# All tests run in the POSIX locale. Focused on the options, variables,
# statements and functions the page defines; not an exhaustive grammar test.
# The standard input of every awk run that could read it is a pipe that ends
# or /dev/null.

assertion  XCU.awk.001
anchor     utilities/awk.html#OPTIONS/-F
anchor     utilities/awk.html#OPTIONS/-f
anchor     utilities/awk.html#OPTIONS/-v
class      required
since      1992
says       -F sepstring sets the field separator, -v assigns before BEGIN with escape processing, and several -f files are concatenated into one program (a - file being standard input).
test       t/001-options.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.002
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/awk.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       BEGIN actions run first in order, then each record is matched against every pattern in order, then END actions; a missing pattern matches all and a missing action prints the record; BEGIN-only programs read no input.
test       t/002-program-structure.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.003
anchor     utilities/awk.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       Records split into fields $1.., $0 is the record; assigning a field rebuilds $0 with OFS, assigning past the end extends NF, and reading a missing field creates none.
test       t/003-fields.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.004
anchor     utilities/awk.html#OPTIONS/-F
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       FS of a blank splits on runs of blanks, another single character at each occurrence, longer values are EREs, and a new FS applies from the next record.
test       t/004-field-separator.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.005
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/awk.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       RS of one character ends records there; a null RS separates records at blank lines and makes newline a field separator.
test       t/005-record-separator.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.006
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       NR, FNR, FILENAME, NF, OFS, ORS, OFMT, CONVFMT, SUBSEP, ENVIRON, ARGC, ARGV, RSTART and RLENGTH have the values and effects described.
test       t/006-special-variables.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.007
anchor     utilities/awk.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       A name=value operand assigns the variable just before the next file is read (after BEGIN, before END), with escapes processed and numeric-string typing.
test       t/007-assignment-operands.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.008
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       Operators follow the stated precedence and associativity; conversions, Boolean contexts, increments, assignment operators, comparison rules, ~, in and concatenation behave as described.
test       t/008-expressions.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.009
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       Arrays are associative with string subscripts; a list of subscripts is joined by SUBSEP; a reference creates an element but in does not; delete, for-in and split work as described.
test       t/009-arrays.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.010
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
since      1992
says       if, while, do-while, for, break, continue, next and exit behave as described; exit runs END actions and sets the status, and an exit inside END ends the program.
test       t/010-control-flow.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.011
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       print and printf write to standard output, a truncated file, an appended file or a pipe, use OFS and ORS, and printf supports d, s, c, x, X, o, u, f, e, g, % with flags, width and precision (also from arguments).
test       t/011-output.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.012
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       length, index, substr, sub, gsub (including & and its escape), sprintf, tolower and toupper return the specified values; sub and gsub act on $0 or a field and re-split.
test       t/012-string-functions.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.013
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       int, sqrt, exp, log, sin, cos and atan2 return the specified values; srand sets a repeatable seed and returns the previous one, and rand returns values in [0,1).
test       t/013-math-functions.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.014
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       getline in its forms reads the next record into $0 or a variable from the input, a file or a command, returning 1, 0 or -1 and updating NR, FNR and NF as described; close and system return statuses.
test       t/014-getline-and-system.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.015
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       User-defined functions may be called before definition and recurse; extra parameters are locals; scalars pass by value and arrays by reference; return needs no value.
test       t/015-user-functions.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.016
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       ERE constants and dynamic regular expressions follow the stated escapes; a bare /re/ tests $0; range patterns are inclusive, repeat, and run to the end if never closed.
test       t/016-regex-and-ranges.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.017
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       Numeric-looking input from fields, getline, ARGV, ENVIRON, split, -v and operands compares as numbers, while string constants and concatenations compare as strings.
test       t/017-numeric-strings.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.018
anchor     utilities/awk.html#CONSEQUENCES OF ERRORS
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
since      1992
says       A file operand that cannot be accessed makes awk write a diagnostic, terminate without further action and exit above zero.
test       t/018-missing-file.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.019
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       nextfile abandons the current file, fflush returns 0, and FILENAME is - for standard input.
test       t/019-later-additions.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.020
anchor     utilities/awk.html#Lexical Conventions
anchor     utilities/awk.html#Grammar
class      required
since      1992
says       A comment runs to the end of the line and has no effect, a backslash-
           newline has no effect, and a newline may follow a comma, an open
           brace, the logical AND and OR operators, the do and else keywords and
           the closing parenthesis of an if, for or while statement.
test       t/020-comments-newlines.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.021
anchor     utilities/awk.html#Regular Expressions
anchor     utilities/awk.html#Lexical Conventions
class      required
since      1992
says       A string constant takes the escapes for alert, backspace, form-feed,
           newline, carriage return, tab, vertical tab, double quote, backslash
           and octal ddd with the longest one to three digits; in regular
           expressions the escapes also work inside brackets, a slash is written
           \/, and a backslash before a special character makes it literal, in
           constants and in dynamic strings.
test       t/021-escape-sequences.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.022
anchor     utilities/awk.html#Regular Expressions
class      required
since      1992
says       With a record separator other than newline an ERE can match a newline
           inside a record, with the default separator no expression matches
           across the separator, and against an ordinary string any character,
           newline and the separator included, can be matched.
test       t/022-newline-in-matching.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.023
anchor     utilities/awk.html#Lexical Conventions
class      required
since      1992
says       Numeric constants take the forms of decimal floating constants written
           with a period (1. .5 1e3 1E-2 2.E1) or plain decimal digits, which are
           decimal even with a leading zero.
test       t/023-numeric-constants.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.024
anchor     utilities/awk.html#Lexical Conventions
class      required
since      1992
says       A word is made of underscores, digits and letters, so a name that
           merely starts with a keyword or built-in function name is an ordinary
           name; a built-in function name may be followed by blanks before its
           parenthesis and a user function is called with the parenthesis right
           after the name.
test       t/024-words-and-tokens.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.025
anchor     utilities/awk.html#Lexical Conventions
anchor     utilities/awk.html#Grammar
class      required
since      1992
says       A slash where division could appear is division (and /= an
           assignment), elsewhere it starts an ERE; the longest token is taken so
           that x+++y is x++ + y; an else goes with the nearest preceding if.
test       t/025-slash-and-longest-token.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.026
anchor     utilities/awk.html#Expressions in awk
class      required
since      1992
says       Post-increment and post-decrement give the old numeric value and pre-
           increment and pre-decrement the new one, for numbers, uninitialized
           variables, strings converted to numbers, array elements and fields.
test       t/026-increment-operands.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.027
anchor     utilities/awk.html#Expressions in awk
class      required
since      1992
says       Text counts as a number when it reads as a decimal number with an
           optional sign and optional blanks around it, possibly with a period or
           exponent, and otherwise is a string, for fields and records.
test       t/027-numeric-string-forms.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.028
anchor     utilities/awk.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       A valid program with no patterns and no actions does not read its file
           operands and awk exits with status 0.
test       t/028-no-actions.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.029
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       Records of up to {LINE_MAX} bytes are supported with a newline or
           another character as separator, and print and printf can write
           {LINE_MAX} bytes.
test       t/029-line-max.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.030
anchor     utilities/awk.html#ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
class      required
since      1992
says       Commands run by system, by output pipes and by command input are found
           through PATH and see the environment that awk started with.
test       t/030-commands-environment.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.031
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       Output files and pipes with textually identical names are one stream
           and names that differ even by a blank are different streams.
test       t/031-stream-names.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.032
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       An unset variable, array element, missing function argument or missing
           field is 0 as a number and empty as a string; referring to a missing
           field creates nothing, but assigning past the last field creates empty
           fields in between.
test       t/032-uninitialized.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.033
anchor     utilities/awk.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       Elements of ARGV are processed as operands after BEGIN: a null element
           is skipped, standard input is read when no file was given, an element
           added in BEGIN is read, an assignment element is applied when reached,
           and a deleted element is skipped.
test       t/033-argv-iteration.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.034
anchor     utilities/awk.html#OPTIONS
class      required
since      1992
says       -F and -v take their argument attached or separate, repeated -v works,
           -f can have its argument attached, and -- ends the options before the
           program.
test       t/034-option-syntax.sh

assertion  XCU.awk.035
anchor     utilities/awk.html#Expressions in awk
class      required
since      1992
says       The operators <, <=, >, >=, == and != compare numbers numerically,
           strings by collation and numeric strings from input numerically, and a
           string constant forces a string comparison.
test       t/035-comparison-operators.sh
